---
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import Icon from '../../components/Icon.astro';

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'How do multiple AI agents edit the same file without conflicts?',
    a: 'CAS edits made through ostk fs_ops include the text the agent expects. If the file changed, non-overlapping edits can auto-merge, nearby edits return a suggested merge, and structural conflicts fail for review. Native client writes and raw shell writes bypass this CAS contract.',
    link: '/docs/coordination-primitives',
  },
  {
    q: 'How do I install ostk?',
    a: 'One command: curl -fsSL https://ostk.ai/install | sh. The script detects your platform (macOS arm64/x86_64, Linux arm64/x86_64), downloads the correct binary, verifies its GPG signature, and places it in your $PATH. Then run ostk init and ostk boot.',
    link: '/start',
  },
  {
    q: 'Do I need to modify my AI agents to work with ostk?',
    a: 'You keep the client UI you already use, but the integration must be configured. MCP-capable clients launch ostk connect and use the ostk-namespaced tools for audited reads, CAS edits, and policy checks. A client\'s native filesystem or shell tools remain outside those guarantees.',
    link: '/docs/coordination-primitives/#vfs-the-observation-substrate',
  },
  {
    q: 'How do I keep API keys safe from AI agents?',
    a: 'Secret values never enter the LLM context. The kernel resolves keys from your platform keychain (macOS Keychain, Linux secret-tool) at call time and injects them via subprocess environment variables. All tool output passes through a masking layer that replaces any secret values before the LLM sees them.',
    link: '/docs/security/#secrets-management-masking',
  },
    {
    q: 'What is my trust tier?',
    a: 'Trust is determined by GPG cross-signatures. T0: your GPG key fingerprint is hardcoded in the kernel trust anchors (full governance — not reachable by ceremony). T1: your key is cross-signed by a T0 holder (write access). T2: GPG key present, not cross-signed (read + narrow write exceptions). T3: no GPG key (boots, reads everything, writes denied by pin caps). Run ostk boot to see your tier.',
    link: '/docs/security/#gpg-rooted-trust-tiers',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can AI agents work on the same repository without conflicts?',
    a: 'Yes, when their writes go through ostk. Kernel-mediated CAS edits use generation history and expected text to detect stale or overlapping work. Non-overlapping changes can auto-merge; ambiguous conflicts fail visibly. Native client writes are still ordinary filesystem writes and require normal Git or workspace coordination.',
    link: '/docs/coordination-primitives',
  },
  {
    q: 'How do I track what changes AI agents made?',
    a: 'Kernel-mediated tool dispatches append timestamped, attributed rows to .ostk/journal.jsonl. Rows are hash-chained and are Ed25519-signed when a signing key is available. Native client actions that bypass ostk do not become ostk audit rows; inspect and verify the journal when audit coverage matters.',
    link: '/docs/architecture/#the-unix-substrate',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does ostk require a cloud connection?',
    a: 'ostk is local-first by custody: canonical project state and page content stay on local disk, and no ostk account or hosted control plane is required. Configured model providers, web/download tools, updates, registrars, outbound drivers, and provider-backed context features can use the network. Anchored Anthropic sessions can automatically make best-effort Anthropic Files copies for context efficiency or cross-host recovery. Operational telemetry stays local.',
    link: '/why',
  },
  {
    q: 'What happens if an agent crashes?',
    a: 'For anchored ostk-managed sessions, turn boundaries write best-effort local drain snapshots. On restart, durable registries rebuild from their local JSONL sources and eligible managed sessions can rehydrate from a compatible snapshot. Work after the last completed snapshot, runtime-only state, and sessions owned by an external harness have separate recovery boundaries.',
    link: '/docs/agent-lifecycle',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is a needle?',
    a: 'A needle is an atomic, executable work item with acceptance criteria. It is the unit of work in ostk, stored in .ostk/needles/issues.jsonl. Create one with ostk work add --priority P0 "title". Needles have priority tiers (P0-P3), status tracking, and dependency links.',
    link: '/docs/commands',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is tack?',
    a: 'Tack is the operator intent language in the TUI. You type commands into the tack bar: :compile dispatches to the kernel, .? queries the OS through the LLM, →573 references a needle, and bare text is filed as hay (raw unstructured intent for later compilation into needles).',
    link: '/docs/tack-grammar',
  },
  {
    q: 'How does ostk differ from Cursor, Devin, or Aider?',
    a: 'ostk is a project-state and coordination layer, not an IDE or model. Verified integrations can use the same local task, decision, audit, and CAS surfaces through ostk. Provider routes and external harnesses have separate compatibility boundaries, so check the support matrix instead of assuming every client or model behaves identically.',
    link: '/about/',
  },
];
---

<DocsLayout
  title="FAQ"
  seoTitle="ostk FAQ for AI Coding Agent Projects"
  description="Answers about ostk project state, AI coding agent handoffs, supported integrations, local custody, installation, coordination, security, and recovery."
  sectionLabel="REFERENCE // FAQ"
>
  <p class="font-headline text-lg text-on-surface-variant italic leading-relaxed max-w-xl mb-12">
    Common questions from developers evaluating or getting started with ostk.
  </p>

  <div class="space-y-px">
    {faqs.map((faq, i) => (
      <details class="group bg-surface-container">
        <summary class="flex items-start gap-4 px-6 py-4 cursor-pointer hover:bg-surface-container-high transition-none">
          <span class="serif-text text-lg font-bold text-primary mt-0.5">{String(i + 1).padStart(2, '0')}</span>
          <span class="font-body text-sm text-on-surface flex-1">{faq.q}</span>
          <Icon name="chevron_right" size="14" class="text-outline group-open:rotate-90 transition-transform duration-100 mt-1" />
        </summary>
        <div class="px-6 pb-5 pl-16">
          <p class="font-body text-xs text-secondary leading-relaxed max-w-xl mb-3">{faq.a}</p>
          <a href={faq.link} class="font-label text-[10px] text-primary uppercase tracking-widest hover:underline">
            READ_MORE →
          </a>
        </div>
      </details>
    ))}
  </div>

  <!-- Glossary -->
  <section class="mt-20">
    <h2 class="section-label mb-6">GLOSSARY</h2>
    <p class="font-body text-xs text-secondary mb-6">Key terms used throughout the documentation.</p>
    <div class="bg-surface-container-low p-6 border-l-2 border-primary">
      {[
        { term: 'needle', def: 'An atomic, executable work item with acceptance criteria. Stored as YAML frontmatter + markdown in .ostk/needles/. The unit of work in ostk.' },
        { term: 'hay', def: 'Raw unstructured intent captured for later compilation into needles. Filed via bare text in the tack bar or ostk hay "...".' },
        { term: 'tack', def: 'The operator intent language used in the TUI. Commands prefixed with : dispatch to the kernel. Bare text files as hay.' },
        { term: 'drain snapshot', def: 'Best-effort local snapshot for an anchored ostk-managed session. Contains conversation history, token accounting, and compatible config for recovery after a completed turn.' },
        { term: 'anchor', def: 'The long-lived daemon process started by ostk listen. Holds the socket, process table, and scheduler. Survives client disconnects.' },
        { term: 'lineage', def: 'A stable identity for an ongoing piece of work (e.g., "scheduler", "worker-a"). Outlives both the client that spawned it and the anchor that ran it.' },
        { term: 'FCP', def: 'File Context Protocol — the interface for drivers that extend the kernel with domain capabilities. MCP servers that let LLMs interact with complex file formats.' },
        { term: 'Hot PR', def: 'The kernel\'s 3-tier conflict resolution system. Tier 1: auto-merge (non-overlapping). Tier 2: mechanical rebase (nearby). Tier 3: manual escalation.' },
        { term: 'generation counter', def: 'A monotonic counter updated by kernel-mediated file operations. CAS edits combine it with expected text to detect stale or conflicting work.' },
        { term: 'bail', def: 'A portable, signed OS bundle. Named for a bail of hay — what you extract from a haystack. Contains public identity (.primefile, ENTITYFILE), boot state, and optionally encrypted internal kernel state or selective exported content. Three modes: public, full, export. See /docs/security/#signed-portable-state-bail.' },
        { term: 'fold(journal.jsonl)', def: 'Replaying supported audit events to rebuild durable registries and projections. Session runtime and some derived indexes have separate recovery paths.' },
        { term: '.language', def: 'A pipe-delimited table of verbs, their tiers, layers, and usage momentum. The kernel loads high-momentum verbs at boot and demand-spawns the rest.' },
        { term: 'sub-stack', def: 'An isolated group of sessions with its own audit log, drain directory, token budget, and IPC channels. Created by the scheduler for multi-worker tasks.' },
        { term: 'OCC', def: 'Optimistic Concurrency Control for kernel-mediated CAS edits. It detects stale expected text or overlapping changes without holding a long-lived file lock.' },
      ].map(g => (
        <div class="flex gap-6 text-xs font-body py-3 border-b border-surface-variant">
          <code class="text-primary w-40 shrink-0">{g.term}</code>
          <span class="text-secondary">{g.def}</span>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>

  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify({
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "FAQPage",
    "mainEntity": faqs.map(f => ({
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": f.q,
      "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": f.a }
    }))
  })} />
</DocsLayout>
